\section{This is not the same solution as classic RAS/IPF}{

Only \code{\link[=bras_calibrate_raking]{bras_calibrate_raking()}} minimizes the same multiplicative
distance as classic RAS. Without bounds, it gives the same matrix as
\code{\link[=ras]{ras()}}, up to numerical tolerance. \code{calfun = "logit"} (this function)
and \code{calfun = "linear"} (\code{\link[=bras_calibrate_linear]{bras_calibrate_linear()}}) minimize other
distance functions (a bounded logistic one and a least-squares one).
Without bounds, both still meet the same margins as classic RAS, but
they generally return a different matrix. See
\code{vignette("RASbound-diagnostics")}.
}

\section{Known fragility on real large tables}{

On a real 57 x 57 input-output-style table, all three
\verb{bras_calibrate_*()} wrappers failed inside \code{survey::calibrate()}, while
the \verb{bras_freeze_*()} functions converged. Removing the zero-target rows
and columns did not fix this. The cause is not known. Differences in
scale between cells are one possibility that has not been tested. If a
wrapper fails on your data, use \code{\link[=bras_freeze_sim]{bras_freeze_sim()}}, \code{\link[=bras_freeze_seq]{bras_freeze_seq()}}
or \code{\link[=bras_freeze_hybrid]{bras_freeze_hybrid()}}.
}

\section{Logit calibration cannot represent an unbounded cell}{

The logit calibration of \code{survey} maps each weight onto the interval
\verb{(lower, upper)} with a logistic transform. That transform is not
defined for an infinite bound, and \code{survey} stops with the error
\code{"Logit calibration requires finite bounds"}. The other functions of
this package use \code{upper = Inf} for "no upper bound". So this wrapper
replaces every infinite upper bound by
\code{unbounded_mult * max(row_targets, col_targets)} (20 times by default).
No cell can reach such a value, and it is far enough from the
saturation zone of the logistic transform. It also scales with the
marginal totals, not with the matrix size.

A tighter replacement is not used. The exact maximum of a cell,
\code{min(row_targets[i], col_targets[j])}, is correct but numerically
fragile: a bound that close to the true supremum is where the logistic
transform saturates and the Newton-Raphson iterations stall (\code{survey}
then reports \code{"Failed to converge"}). Increase \code{unbounded_mult} if that
still happens on your data. An infinite lower bound (unusual, since the
default is \code{0}) is replaced by a large negative number.
}

\section{Failure mode}{
 a cell at a real, tight bound:
Suppose a cell has a real, tight \code{upper} and its seed in \code{x0} is at or
above it. The seed is moved to exactly that bound before the
calibration starts (see \code{vignette("RASbound-diagnostics")}). If most
other cells have no bound and get the replacement above, one cell sits
on a tight bound next to cells whose bound is tens of times larger.
This combination can make the Newton-Raphson solve diverge, with the
error \code{"infinite or missing values"} from an internal routine, instead
of converging slowly. \code{vignette("RASbound-diagnostics")} has a
reproducible example.

If you know that some cells need a tight bound, give real finite bounds
to the other cells too, even generous ones, instead of relying on the
replacement of \code{Inf}. Or use \code{\link[=bras_calibrate_raking]{bras_calibrate_raking()}} or
\code{\link[=bras_calibrate_linear]{bras_calibrate_linear()}}. Both accept \code{Inf} and need no replacement,
so they are more robust when most cells are effectively unbounded.
}

